  "generated_text": "1. First, identify the coordinates of points B and C.\n2. Since B is directly below A and C is directly below B, we can use the distance formula to find the length of BC.\n3. The distance formula between two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n   \\]\n4. Substitute the coordinates of B and C:\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{((-16) - 0)^2 + (0 - 12)^2}\n   \\]\n5. Simplify inside the square root:\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{(-16)^2 + (-12)^2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{256 + 144}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   BC = \\sqrt{400}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of segment BC is \\(\\boxed{20}\\) units.",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[20, '20']",
    "gold_answer": "Looking at triangle $ABD$, we can calculate $AB$ through the Pythagorean Theorem. \\begin{align*}\nAB&=\\sqrt{37^2-(19+16)^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{37^2-35^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{(37+35)(37-35)}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{72 \\cdot 2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{36 \\cdot 4}=6 \\cdot 2=12\\end{align*} Using Pythagorean theorem again to find $BC$, we get \\begin{align*}\nBC&=\\sqrt{12^2+16^2}\\\\\n&=\\sqrt{4^2(3^2+4^2)}\\\\\n&=4\\sqrt{9+16}\\\\\n&=4\\sqrt{25}=4 \\cdot 5=\\boxed{20} \\text{ units}.\\end{align*}",
    "is_correct": true
  },
